  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › Basic Spanish words</p>
  <h1>Basic Spanish words for beginners</h1>
  <p class="lede">These 15 basic Spanish words are the ones you will use every single day. Spanish spelling matches its sounds almost perfectly, so once you learn to say these words, you can read them too — and use them from your very first day.</p>

  <h2>15 basic Spanish words</h2><table class="word-table">
      <tr><th>Spanish</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Hola</td><td>Hello</td><td>Hola, ¿cómo estás? — Hello, how are you?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Buenos días</td><td>Good morning</td><td>Buenos días, señor. — Good morning, sir.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Buenas tardes</td><td>Good afternoon</td><td>Buenas tardes a todos. — Good afternoon, everyone.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Buenas noches</td><td>Good evening / good night</td><td>Buenas noches, hasta mañana. — Good night, see you tomorrow.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gracias</td><td>Thank you</td><td>Muchas gracias. — Thank you very much.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Por favor</td><td>Please</td><td>Un café, por favor. — A coffee, please.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>De nada</td><td>You are welcome</td><td>—Gracias. —De nada. —Thank you. —You are welcome.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sí</td><td>Yes</td><td>Sí, claro. — Yes, of course.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No, gracias. — No, thank you.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lo siento</td><td>I am sorry</td><td>Lo siento, no entiendo. — I am sorry, I do not understand.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Perdón</td><td>Excuse me / sorry</td><td>Perdón, ¿dónde está el baño? — Excuse me, where is the bathroom?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Adiós</td><td>Goodbye</td><td>Adiós, hasta luego. — Goodbye, see you later.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hasta luego</td><td>See you later</td><td>Hasta luego, amigo. — See you later, friend.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>¿Cómo estás?</td><td>How are you?</td><td>¿Cómo estás hoy? — How are you today?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bien</td><td>Well / fine</td><td>Muy bien, gracias. — Very well, thank you.</td></tr>
    </table>
  <h2>How to use these words</h2>
  <p>The words in this list do most of the social work in Spanish. With just hola, gracias, por favor, lo siento and perdón, you can be polite in almost any situation. Add buenos días in the morning, buenas tardes in the afternoon and buenas noches in the evening — Spanish speakers notice and appreciate the right greeting for the time of day.</p>
  <p>Perdón and lo siento both mean “sorry”, but they are used differently. Perdón is for getting attention or a small bump — “excuse me”. Lo siento is for a real apology — “I am sorry”. In daily life, perdón is the one you will use most.</p>
  <h2>From words to your first conversation</h2>
  <p>Put the words together and you already have a conversation: “Hola, buenos días. ¿Cómo estás?” — “Muy bien, gracias. ¿Y tú?” — “Bien, gracias.” That is a real exchange, and it uses only words from this list.</p>
  <p>The next step is learning the most common verbs — ser, estar, tener, ir — and the present tense, so you can say what you do, where you are, and what you want. Our Spanish learning guide takes you through that, step by step.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Saying “buenas noches” in the morning — it means good evening or good night, not good morning.</li>
    <li>Using “adiós” for every goodbye — friends usually say “hasta luego” (see you later) or “chao”.</li>
    <li>Learning words alone, without the sentences they belong in.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the upside-down question mark ¿ at the start of a question — Spanish writes it at both ends.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn 5 words a day and use each one in a sentence the same day.</li>
    <li>Read the words aloud — Spanish spelling makes this the easiest win in any language.</li>
    <li>Listen for these words in Spanish songs and shows; hearing them in real speech makes them stick.</li>
    <li>Practise the mini-conversation above until you can say it without thinking.</li>
  </ul>
