  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How many Spanish words do I need to start speaking?</summary><p>You can start with these 15 words plus a few verbs. With 100 words you can greet people, order food and ask simple questions. With 500–1,000 words you can manage most everyday situations — and Spanish gives you thousands of words free, because so many look like English words.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is Spanish pronunciation hard?</summary><p>No — it is one of the easiest parts of Spanish. Each letter almost always makes the same sound, and there are only five vowel sounds. Once you learn the sounds, you can read any Spanish word out loud, even if you do not know what it means yet.</p></details>
  <details><summary>What is the difference between perdón and lo siento?</summary><p>Perdón is “excuse me” — getting someone’s attention, passing by, or a small mistake. Lo siento is “I am sorry” — a real apology. Use perdón in daily life; save lo siento for when you have actually done something wrong.</p></details>
  <details><summary>How do I say “good night” in Spanish?</summary><p>Buenas noches — it means both “good evening” and “good night”. Use it from the evening onwards, including when you go to bed. Buenos días is only for the morning.</p></details>
